WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.119
In a decoupled site, Orchard Core manages the content, and your own Razor Pages render it.

00:00:07.119 --> 00:00:11.772
In this video, you add Orchard Core to a Razor Pages project, model

00:00:11.772 --> 00:00:15.660
a blog post, load it in a page, and preview your edits.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:25.318
Decoupled is a development model where the front end and the administration run

00:00:25.318 --> 00:00:30.317
in the same web application, and only the administration is driven by the CMS.

00:00:30.317 --> 00:00:34.780
Orchard Core authors the content, and your code decides how it looks.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:41.717
You write ASP.NET Razor Pages or controllers, and load content items from Orchard Core.

00:00:41.717 --> 00:00:48.237
This video uses Razor Pages, and much of it applies to an existing ASP.NET Core application.

00:00:48.237 --> 00:00:51.630
A separate video covers a headless front end.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.787
Three terms matter:

00:00:53.787 --> 00:00:57.952
a content type defines the properties of your content, like a class.

00:00:57.952 --> 00:01:01.273
A content part is a building block you add to a type.

00:01:01.273 --> 00:01:05.720
A content item is a versioned document based on a type, like an instance.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:17.383
Create a project with the dotnet new webapp command, and add the OrchardCore.Application.Cms.Core.Targets package.

00:01:17.383 --> 00:01:20.368
It holds the minimum for a decoupled site:

00:01:20.368 --> 00:01:26.960
the administration theme TheAdmin, the Blank site and Headless site recipes, and no front-end theme.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:32.471
The bare minimum Program.cs calls AddOrchardCms on the services.

00:01:32.471 --> 00:01:38.320
Then it calls UseStaticFiles and UseOrchardCore on the application, and runs it.

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:43.840
Remove the lines the template added for Razor Pages and routing:

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:51.268
AddRazorPages, UseRouting, UseAuthorization and MapRazorPages, with its error handling and HTTPS lines.

00:01:51.268 --> 00:01:55.220
Orchard Core sets up Razor Pages and routing itself.

00:01:59.463 --> 00:02:03.672
Start the application, and the Setup screen asks a few questions.

00:02:03.672 --> 00:02:08.533
Enter a name for the site, which is used as the default title of your pages.

00:02:08.987 --> 00:02:11.735
In the Recipe list, choose Blank site.

00:02:11.735 --> 00:02:18.417
It enables the content management features and sets no front-end theme, which suits a decoupled site.

00:02:18.883 --> 00:02:24.251
Pick a Default Time Zone, which sets how dates and times are displayed and edited.

00:02:24.251 --> 00:02:28.400
The database type is Sqlite, which needs no connection string.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.853
Then enter a user name, an email and a password for the Super User.

00:02:33.603 --> 00:02:34.934
Click Finish Setup.

00:02:34.934 --> 00:02:38.929
After a few seconds, the site displays the home page of the template.

00:02:38.929 --> 00:02:43.563
By default, the data of the site is stored in the App_Data folder of the project.

00:02:47.798 --> 00:02:52.952
In the administration, open Design, then Content Definition, then Content Types.

00:02:52.952 --> 00:02:56.608
This is where you define the content types of the site.

00:02:57.115 --> 00:02:58.893
Click Create new type.

00:02:58.893 --> 00:03:04.420
Enter Blog Post as the Display Name, and the Technical Name fills in as BlogPost.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:05.945
Then click Create.

00:03:06.412 --> 00:03:09.670
Content parts are the building blocks of a type.

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:13.712
Select Title and MarkdownBody, then click Add Selected Parts.

00:03:14.239 --> 00:03:16.004
The type lists its parts.

00:03:16.004 --> 00:03:21.869
Title provides a title for the content item, and MarkdownBody provides a Markdown formatted body.

00:03:26.169 --> 00:03:30.485
Open Content, then Content Items, and click New Blog Post.

00:03:30.485 --> 00:03:34.329
The editor shows the Title and MarkdownBody fields.

00:03:34.809 --> 00:03:38.311
Enter a title and some Markdown, and click Publish.

00:03:38.311 --> 00:03:43.349
A notification confirms it, and the Content Items list shows your blog post.

00:03:47.549 --> 00:03:53.415
Add BlogPost.cshtml to the Pages folder, with a route that takes an identifier.

00:03:53.415 --> 00:03:57.694
Inject IOrchardHelper, and call GetContentItemByIdAsync.

00:03:57.694 --> 00:04:03.699
It returns the published version of the content item, or null when there is none.

00:04:04.449 --> 00:04:09.045
The identifier of a content item stays the same across its versions.

00:04:09.045 --> 00:04:13.269
Copy it from the address of the edit page, after ContentItems.

00:04:13.269 --> 00:04:16.189
The DisplayText property holds the title.

00:04:16.939 --> 00:04:21.533
The properties that each type defines are in Content, a dynamic document.

00:04:21.533 --> 00:04:27.873
ConsoleLog writes the content item to the browser console, and writes null in a production environment.

00:04:27.873 --> 00:04:30.839
MarkdownBodyPart holds the Markdown property.

00:04:31.589 --> 00:04:38.083
Read the Markdown from the Content property, and convert it to HTML with MarkdownToHtmlAsync.

00:04:38.083 --> 00:04:41.429
The conversion sanitizes the HTML by default.

00:04:43.047 --> 00:04:46.155
Open the page with the identifier of your blog post.

00:04:46.155 --> 00:04:49.477
It shows the title, and the body rendered from Markdown.

00:04:53.707 --> 00:04:56.882
An identifier is not friendly in an address.

00:04:56.882 --> 00:05:01.326
The Alias part lets a content item have a readable text instead.

00:05:01.326 --> 00:05:06.977
On the Blog Post type, click Add Parts, select Alias, and click Add Selected Parts.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:09.088
Edit the blog post.

00:05:09.088 --> 00:05:11.080
The Alias field is there:

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:14.680
type an alias, such as pruning, and click Publish.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:21.858
In the page, take a slug in the route, and call GetContentItemByHandleAsync with

00:05:21.858 --> 00:05:25.230
a handle made of alias, a colon, and the slug.

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:32.310
Open the page with the alias, and the same blog post appears at a readable address.

00:05:33.908 --> 00:05:36.710
The Alias part can also generate the alias.

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:38.539
Edit the part on the type:

00:05:38.539 --> 00:05:42.942
its Pattern is a Liquid template, and by default it applies the slugify

00:05:42.942 --> 00:05:44.828
filter to the display text.

00:05:45.314 --> 00:05:49.342
Edit the blog post, clear the Alias field, and click Publish.

00:05:49.342 --> 00:05:53.610
The field says to leave it empty to re-generate it, and the alias

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:55.774
becomes the slug of the title.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:04.191
Editors click Preview to see the content item as they edit it.

00:06:04.191 --> 00:06:09.270
Without more setup, Orchard Core renders a generic page, not your Razor Page.

00:06:09.739 --> 00:06:13.527
To use your page, add the Preview part to the type, and edit it.

00:06:13.527 --> 00:06:17.748
Its Pattern is a Liquid template that produces the address of your page:

00:06:17.748 --> 00:06:21.049
the blog post route, followed by the alias of the item.

00:06:21.049 --> 00:06:22.239
Then click Save.

00:06:27.873 --> 00:06:31.698
Click Preview again, and the window renders your Razor Page.

00:06:31.698 --> 00:06:36.161
Change the title in the editor, and the preview updates a moment later,

00:06:36.161 --> 00:06:40.333
because your page loads the edited item in place of the saved one.

00:06:41.933 --> 00:06:44.828
A page can also tell when it renders a preview.

00:06:44.828 --> 00:06:50.345
Orchard Core marks the request with ContentPreviewFeature, and its Previewing property is true,

00:06:50.345 --> 00:06:52.803
so the page can show hints for editors.

00:06:57.003 --> 00:07:01.099
You added Orchard Core to a Razor Pages project, set it up with

00:07:01.099 --> 00:07:04.244
the Blank site recipe, and modeled a blog post.

00:07:04.244 --> 00:07:08.874
Your pages load content items by identifier or by alias, and the Preview

00:07:08.874 --> 00:07:11.663
part points the preview at your own page.

00:07:13.663 --> 00:07:19.370
Orchard Core stores and edits the content, and your Razor Pages decide how it appears.

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:20.983
Thanks for watching.

